---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import coverage from '../data/coverage.json';
import { REPO, PKG, VERSION } from '../lib/site';

const rows = coverage.rows;
const ranDate = (coverage.generated || '').slice(0, 10);
// Display the major.minor (e.g. "1.2") so the install button tracks VERSION.
const shortVersion = VERSION.split('.').slice(0, 2).join('.');

// Four-layer model (design §3). Layer 1 shipped in 1.0, Layer 2 in 1.1,
// Layer 3 in 1.2, extended in 1.3, Layer 4 in 1.4 (all 'released', log-only).
// Non-active layers are dimmed so the page never implies they are in 1.0.
const layers = [
  { n: 1, name: 'Request parsing', covers: 'Upload/multipart floods (PHP2Shell), input-variable floods, deeply nested arrays, oversized headers — refused while PHP parses the request.', status: 'active' },
  { n: 2, name: 'Request inspection', covers: 'Content inspection of decoded GET/POST/cookie/path values for SQL injection, path traversal and command/code injection. It detects and logs; it does not block by default.', status: 'released' },
  { n: 3, name: 'Runtime checks', covers: 'Sink hardening: guards command execution, unserialize, include and eval with a flat allowlist policy, plus phar:// and an SSRF egress guard (both off by default, new in 1.3). It detects and logs; it does not block by default.', status: 'released' },
  { n: 4, name: 'After a breach', covers: 'Watches PHP run from upload directories, unexpected binaries, unexpected outbound hosts and .php writes. It detects and logs; it does not block by default, and does nothing until you set its lists.', status: 'released' },
];

// Comparison facts compiled Oct 2026 from each project's own documentation.
// Sources are cited as plain text (no external requests; see the methodology note).
const compareAsOf = 'October 2026';
const compare = [
  { name: 'Howdah', self: true, where: 'Inside PHP (Rust extension)', license: 'MIT OR Apache-2.0', job: 'Refuses Layer-1 request-shape & resource-exhaustion floods during parsing', eol: 'Yes — 5.4 to 8.5, including EOL branches', ext: 'None' },
  { name: 'Snuffleupagus', where: 'Inside PHP (C extension)', license: 'LGPL', job: 'Hardening: kills bug classes + virtual-patching', eol: 'PHP 7.0+ (not 5.x)', ext: 'None' },
  { name: 'OpenRASP (Baidu)', where: 'Inside the app (RASP, hooks sinks)', license: 'Apache-2.0', job: 'Runtime detection of SQL/file/SSRF via sink hooks', eol: 'PHP extension; project quiet since 2022', ext: 'Self-hosted management' },
  { name: 'Aikido Zen', where: 'In-app agent (separate process)', license: 'Open-source agent + commercial cloud', job: 'Hooks runtime sinks (SQLi, SSRF, path/command injection)', eol: 'PHP 7.2+ (not 5.x)', ext: 'Aikido cloud (SaaS)' },
  { name: 'ModSecurity + OWASP CRS', where: 'Web-server module (Apache / Nginx)', license: 'Apache-2.0', job: 'Generic signature WAF at the server layer', eol: 'PHP-agnostic (sits in front of PHP)', ext: 'None' },
  { name: 'Datadog App & API Protection', where: 'In-app (tracing library) + SaaS', license: 'Proprietary (commercial)', job: 'In-app WAF / threat protection with managed rules', eol: 'Per the Datadog PHP library', ext: 'Datadog cloud (paid)' },
];
---
<Layout
  title="Howdah: a defensive firewall for PHP"
  description="Howdah is an open-source, defensive firewall that loads into PHP itself. It blocks abusive upload and input floods before they exhaust memory, including on end-of-life PHP (5.4 to 8.1) that will not get the official fix."
  path="/"
>
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source &middot; defensive &middot; built for PHP 5.4 to 8.5 (see coverage for what is tested)</p>
      <h1>Howdah stops abusive request <em>floods</em> inside PHP, before they exhaust memory.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Upload-part floods are the shape of the PHP2Shell attack. Howdah is a firewall that loads into PHP itself, so it protects even end-of-life PHP (5.4 to 8.1) that will never get the official fix — on the versions tested in the coverage table.</p>
      <p class="fine">Built for sysadmins who run PHP on their own servers, including servers stuck on old PHP. Howdah is defensive only: it recognizes and refuses abusive requests, nothing else.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/install/">Install Howdah {shortVersion}</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/coverage/">See exactly what has been tested</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/demo/">Try the demo</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="what">
    <div class="wrap cols aside">
      <div>
        <h2>What is Howdah, and why the name?</h2>
        <p>Howdah is a native extension for PHP. When a web request arrives, PHP starts parsing it before any of your code runs. Howdah watches that parsing step and refuses requests that are built to exhaust memory, while PHP is still working through them.</p>
        <p>Version 1.0 completes Howdah's first layer: limits on <code>multipart/form-data</code> uploads, on the number and shape of input variables, and on request headers. The critical resource-exhaustion limits block by default, even in detect mode; everything else starts in log-only mode so you can watch it before you let it block. Version 1.1 adds Layer 2, which inspects request content for injection patterns and logs them; it does not block by default. Version 1.2 adds Layer 3, which guards four dangerous PHP sinks, and also logs first. Version 1.3 extends it with <code>phar://</code> and SSRF guards (off by default) and brings <code>include</code> and <code>eval</code> to PHP 5.x. Version 1.4 adds Layer 4, which watches what PHP does after a breach (PHP run from upload directories, unexpected binaries and outbound hosts, <code>.php</code> writes). It also logs first and does nothing until you set its lists.</p>
        <p>It is not a replacement for patching. Upgrade PHP when you can. Use Howdah when you cannot, or until you can.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="card" aria-labelledby="howdah-word">
        <h3 id="howdah-word">A howdah?</h3>
        <p>A howdah is the covered seat strapped onto an elephant's back: a small armoured shelter that carries someone safely. PHP's mascot is an elephant, so the name fits. Howdah sits on PHP's back and shields what is riding on it.</p>
        <p>Howdah is an independent project. It is not made by, or endorsed by, the PHP project.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How it works: four layers</h2>
      <p>Howdah's protection is organised in four layers. <strong>Version 1.0 delivered Layer 1; version 1.1 added Layer 2; version 1.2 added Layer 3, extended in 1.3; version 1.4 added Layer 4.</strong> Layer 2 detects and logs SQL injection, path traversal and command/code injection in request values; Layer 3 guards dangerous sinks (command execution, <code>unserialize</code>, <code>include</code>, <code>eval</code>, and in 1.3 <code>phar://</code> and SSRF); Layer 4 watches what PHP does after a breach (PHP run from upload directories, unexpected binaries and outbound hosts, <code>.php</code> writes). None of the three blocks by default.</p>
      <ol class="layers" aria-label="The four protection layers; a request flows from the top down">
        {layers.map((l) => (
          <li class={l.status}>
            <span class="lnum" aria-hidden="true">{l.n}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>Layer {l.n}: {l.name} {l.status === 'active' ? <span class="pill ok">Active in 1.0</span> : l.status === 'released' ? <span class="pill ok">Active in {l.n === 4 ? '1.4' : l.n === 3 ? '1.2' : '1.1'} · log-only</span> : <span class="pill na">Planned</span>}</h3>
              <p>{l.covers}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="legend">A request enters at Layer 1. Because Howdah runs inside PHP's own request handling, Layer 1 can count upload parts and input variables <em>as PHP reads them</em> and stop the request early — something a PHP-level script or a network box cannot do (see the PHP2Shell story below).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="enforces">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Layer 1 — what Howdah enforces</h2>
      <p>These are the Layer-1 default limits. A request that crosses a limit has the offending data dropped and is logged; in block mode (or for a Critical rule in any mode) it is also refused with HTTP 403/413 and a request ID that matches the log line. Every limit is configurable in a TOML file; the <a href="/config/">config editor</a> builds and validates one in the browser. Numbers below are the shipped defaults.</p>
      <div class="cols two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div>
          <h3>Multipart upload limits</h3>
          <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Multipart limits (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">Multipart upload default limits</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Limit</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Stops</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_parts</th><td>2000</td><td>Floods of form-field parts</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_files</th><td>200</td><td>File-part floods (the PHP2Shell shape)</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_filename_bytes</th><td>512</td><td>Oversized filenames</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_field_name_bytes</th><td>1024</td><td>Oversized field names</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_parse_memory_bytes</th><td>256&nbsp;MiB</td><td>Total body Howdah will process</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="legend">Enforced <strong>while PHP parses the body</strong>, before per-part memory is allocated — the 403/413 lands before your script runs. <code>max_part_headers</code> and <code>max_part_header_bytes</code> also exist but are best-effort (only the <code>Content-Disposition</code> header reaches the extension).</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Input-shape &amp; request-header limits</h3>
          <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Input and header limits (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">Input-shape and request-header default limits</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Limit</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Stops</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_input_vars</th><td>2000</td><td>Variable / hash-collision floods</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_input_nesting_depth</th><td>64</td><td>Deeply nested arrays</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_input_key_bytes</th><td>1024</td><td>Oversized variable names / keys</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_request_headers</th><td>200</td><td>Request-header floods</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">max_request_header_bytes</th><td>16&nbsp;KiB</td><td>Oversized header lines</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="legend"><strong>Honest caveat:</strong> for these, the guaranteed mitigation is that Howdah <strong>drops the offending variable</strong> (bounding the symbol table) and logs it. The 403 is best-effort and may arrive at request shutdown on SAPIs that build superglobals lazily. See the <a href="/docs/configuration/">rule reference</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="legend" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><strong>Modes &amp; severity.</strong> Critical rules (the unambiguous resource-exhaustion shapes) block even in <code>detect</code> mode; Standard rules log in detect and block only in <code>block</code> mode. This is why the recommended &ldquo;start in detect&rdquo; rollout never leaves you open to PHP2Shell during the first hours. Full directive and rule reference: <a href="/docs/configuration/">Configuration</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="inspection">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Layer 2 — what Howdah inspects <span class="pill ok">1.1 · log-only</span></h2>
      <p>Layer 1 limits the <em>shape</em> of a request. Layer 2 looks at the <em>content</em> of decoded request values: GET query parameters, POST fields, cookies and the URL path, through the same request hook Layer 1 uses. <strong>It detects and logs; it does not block by default.</strong></p>
      <div class="cols two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div>
          <h3>Three classes of pattern</h3>
          <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Layer 2 detection classes (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">Layer 2 detection classes with illustrative shapes</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Class</th><th scope="col">Rule name</th><th scope="col">Example shape</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">SQL injection</th><td><code>sql_injection</code></td><td><code>&#39; OR 1=1 --</code></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Path traversal</th><td><code>path_traversal</code></td><td><code>../../etc/passwd</code></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Command / code injection</th><td><code>command_injection</code></td><td><code>; cat /etc/passwd</code></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="legend">The examples illustrate each shape; they are not a complete list of what is matched. Cross-site scripting (XSS) is <strong>not</strong> covered.</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem">How detection works</h3>
          <p>A small, high-confidence signature scan that runs in linear time, so there is no catastrophic backtracking. Work is bounded per value and per request, so the scan itself cannot be turned into a denial of service; values past a budget are not scanned.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Log-first, on purpose</h3>
          <p>Layer 2 has its own <code>inspect.mode</code>, which defaults to <code>detect</code> (log only). It is independent of the global <code>howdah.mode</code>: setting <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> arm it. To block, an operator sets <code>inspect.mode</code>, or one class, to <code>block</code> explicitly.</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem">What a detection looks like</h3>
          <p>One JSON log line carrying the rule class, the source (<code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, <code>cookie</code> or <code>path</code>) and the variable name. The matched value is never logged.</p>
          <pre tabindex="0" data-sample="event"><code>{`{"action":"log","client_ip":"203.0.113.7","layer":2,"method":"GET","reason":"sql_injection pattern in get variable 'q'","request_id":"1a2d239820e2f06bc","rule":"sql_injection","schema":2,"ts":"1792864974146","uri":"/index.php","version":"1.4.0"}`}</code></pre>
          <p class="legend">From a lab run (PHP 7.4, php-fpm, request <code>/index.php?q=' OR 1=1 --</code>). The query string is stripped from the logged <code>uri</code>; for a path detection the path itself is the logged URI. Every field: <a href="/docs/configuration/#log">event log reference</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="legend" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><strong>Honest caveat.</strong> Signature inspection is best-effort and can be bypassed, which is exactly why Layer 2 logs rather than blocks. Use the log to see what is happening, and fix the application (parameterized queries, path normalization, no shell calls on user input). To configure or enable it, see the <code>[inspect]</code> section of the <a href="/docs/configuration/#layer-2-inspection">configuration reference</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sinks" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Layer 3 — the sinks Howdah guards <span class="pill ok">1.3 · log-only</span></h2>
      <p>Layer 2 looks at request values. Layer 3 looks at what the application does with them: it guards dangerous PHP functions, called <em>sinks</em>, with a flat allowlist policy in the style of Snuffleupagus. Version 1.3 <strong>actively guards six sinks. It detects and logs; it does not block by default.</strong> This is sink hardening, not XSS or output inspection.</p>
      <div class="cols two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div>
          <h3>Six active sinks</h3>
          <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Layer 3 active sinks (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">Layer 3 sinks guarded in 1.3</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Sink</th><th scope="col">Functions</th><th scope="col">Default</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Command execution</th><td><code>system</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>shell_exec</code>, <code>passthru</code>, <code>popen</code>, <code>proc_open</code></td><td>Logs when not allowlisted</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Unserialize</th><td><code>unserialize</code></td><td><strong>Off</strong>; opt in to log</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Include</th><td><code>include</code>, <code>require</code></td><td>Inert until <code>allow_paths</code> is set</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Eval</th><td><code>eval</code></td><td>Logs when reached</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Phar</th><td><code>phar://</code> via stream functions and <code>include</code></td><td><strong>Off</strong>; opt in per sink</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">SSRF</th><td><code>file_get_contents</code>, <code>fopen</code>, <code>get_headers</code>, <code>fsockopen</code>, <code>stream_socket_client</code>, curl</td><td><strong>Off</strong>; opt in per sink</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="legend">Known limitation: <code>proc_open</code> with an array command (PHP 7.4+) is not inspected.</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem">Coverage by PHP version</h3>
          <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Layer 3 coverage by PHP version (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">Layer 3 sink coverage by PHP version</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Sink</th><th scope="col">PHP 5.4–5.6</th><th scope="col">PHP 7.0–7.4</th><th scope="col">PHP 8.0–8.5</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Command execution</th><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>unserialize</code> (off by default)</th><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>include</code> / <code>require</code></th><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span> (opcache caveat)</td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>eval</code></th><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>phar://</code> (off by default)</th><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span>, deserialization</td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span>, deserialization</td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span> only (no deserialization claim)</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">SSRF (off by default)</th><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td><td><span class="pill ok">detect + block</span></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="legend">Verified on PHP 5.4, 5.6, 7.4, 8.3 and 8.5; other minors build and are expected to work but are not independently verified. A debug-build leak review is pending. Blocking an include or eval stops it running and the script continues. <strong>PHP 5.x <code>include</code> is checked at compile time, so a file served from a warm opcache is not re-inspected</strong> (<code>eval</code> is unaffected; the 7.0+ opcode handler is not affected by opcache). <strong>Phar:</strong> on PHP 5.x/7.x this covers metadata deserialization via the stream wrapper; on 8.x it is detection and denial of <code>phar://</code> use only. The direct Phar API (<code>new Phar</code>, <code>PharData</code>, <code>Phar::loadPhar</code>, <code>Phar::mapPhar</code>, <code>Phar::getMetadata</code>) is not covered. <strong>SSRF</strong> is best-effort, resolve-at-check-time detection, not a connection-time boundary: DNS rebinding, redirects, <code>curl_multi_*</code>, <code>CURLOPT_RESOLVE</code>/<code>CONNECT_TO</code> and a non-string or absent <code>CURLOPT_URL</code> bypass it, and <code>copy</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>readfile</code>, <code>SoapClient</code>, <code>DOMDocument::load*</code> and <code>simplexml_load_file</code> are not hooked. It adds a DNS lookup per distinct outbound host with no timeout. More detail on the <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Log-first, on purpose</h3>
          <p>Layer 3 has its own <code>sinks.mode</code>, which defaults to <code>detect</code> (log only). It is independent of the global <code>howdah.mode</code>: setting <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> arm sinks. Any active sink can block, and only when an operator sets that sink to <code>block</code> explicitly, after setting its allowlist. The 1.3 <code>phar</code> and <code>ssrf</code> sinks are off by default and only an explicit per-sink mode arms them.</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem">The argument is never logged</h3>
          <p>An event records the sink and the function name (plus the class name for <code>unserialize</code>). The command line and the serialized payload are never written to the log.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="legend" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><strong>Honest caveat.</strong> A flat allowlist policy is best-effort virtual patching and can be bypassed, which is why Layer 3 logs rather than blocks. Use the log to learn what your application really calls, then fix the application (no shell calls on user input, no unserialize of untrusted data). To configure it, see the <code>[sinks]</code> section of the <a href="/docs/configuration/#layer-3-sinks">configuration reference</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="behavior">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Layer 4 — what Howdah watches after a breach <span class="pill ok">1.4 · log-only</span></h2>
      <p>Layers 1 to 3 try to stop the attack. Layer 4 assumes something got through, such as a webshell uploaded into a writable directory, and watches what PHP then does. <strong>It detects and logs; it does not block by default, and it does nothing until you set its lists.</strong> Events name the script that made the call, so a finding points at the webshell even when a legitimate script <code>require</code>d it.</p>
      <div class="cols two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div>
          <h3>Four things it watches</h3>
          <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Layer 4 facets (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">Layer 4 facets in 1.4</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Facet</th><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Flags</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Execution origin</th><td><code>deny_exec_dirs</code></td><td>PHP run from a denied directory, as the requested script or <code>require</code>d by another</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Expected binaries</th><td><code>allow_exec_binaries</code></td><td><code>exec</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>popen</code>, <code>proc_open</code>, <code>shell_exec</code>, <code>passthru</code> to a program not on the list</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Expected egress</th><td><code>allow_egress_hosts</code></td><td>An outbound connection to a host not on the list</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><code>.php</code> writes</th><td><code>deny_php_writes</code></td><td>A PHP file written into a denied directory, or from a script that lives in one</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="legend">Lab-verified on PHP 5.4, 5.6, 7.4, 8.3, 8.4 and 8.5: 27 checks per version, covering each facet in <code>detect</code> and <code>block</code>, that a framework-style view write and a plain read of a <code>.php</code> file are not flagged, and that the default configuration is inert. Other minors are not independently verified. More detail on the <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Hybrid policy: you set it, Howdah helps you find it</h3>
          <p>You supply the allowlists (<code>allow_exec_binaries</code>, <code>allow_egress_hosts</code>) and the denied directories. To learn what your application really does, set <code>observe = true</code>: Howdah logs each would-be finding as a <code>proposed</code> line, such as the binary that was run or the host contacted, which you can fold into the lists. Observe never blocks and never edits your config, and its output needs your review.</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem">Log-first, on purpose</h3>
          <p>Layer 4 has its own <code>behavior.mode</code>, which defaults to <code>detect</code> (log only). It is independent of the global <code>howdah.mode</code>: setting <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> arm it. Every list ships empty, so a fresh install blocks nothing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="legend" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><strong>Honest caveat.</strong> Howdah only sees what PHP itself does: a process PHP launches and then detaches, a reverse shell that forks away, or a dropper that never ran through PHP is invisible to Layer 4. For <code>deny_exec_dirs</code>, the requested-script case does not depend on opcache, but the <code>require</code>d-file case is checked at first compile and is best-effort, so a file served from a warm opcache may not be re-checked. Allowlists are best-effort and can be bypassed. The <code>.php</code>-write facet is scoped by origin and can still flag frameworks that compile templates into a denied directory, so scope it before you use <code>block</code>. Observe only proposes. To configure it, see the <code>[behavior]</code> section of the <a href="/docs/configuration/#layer-4-behavior">configuration reference</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="story">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The PHP2Shell story, plainly told</h2>
      <div class="cols two">
        <div>
          <p>On 21 October 2026 the PHP project patched two linked flaws, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, which security researchers named PHP2Shell. As publicly reported, a crafted <code>multipart/form-data</code> request makes PHP use a lot of memory while it reads the headers of upload parts. A body well under the default 8&nbsp;MB limit can hold hundreds of tiny, empty file parts. Corrupted state from that can, according to the reports, lead to remote code execution without logging in.</p>
          <p>According to public reports, the official fix ships for PHP 8.2 and newer; older branches get no official patch. PHP 8.1 and older still run a very large share of the web.</p>
          <div class="note">
            <h3>What we did and did not verify</h3>
            <p>Our tests reproduce only the harmless <em>structure</em> of such a flood: how many parts and fields, and how large. We did <strong>not</strong> reproduce the CVE's <code>session.upload_progress</code> header-parsing path, so we do <strong>not</strong> claim Howdah blocks CVE-2026-109618 on a stock configuration.</p>
            <p>What we measured: PHP's own <code>max_input_vars</code> and <code>max_file_uploads</code> already keep the flood shape we built small on a default box. On a box where an admin has raised those caps, the same shape used 127 to 173&nbsp;MB, against a 128&nbsp;MB limit, and Howdah's default limits refuse it long before that. Howdah's value is strongest on raised-cap servers and on old PHP with no official fix. See the <a href="/coverage/">coverage table</a> and <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/coverage-data.md`}>the measurements</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why only a PHP extension can stop it</h3>
          <ul class="facts">
            <li>PHP reads the whole upload into <code>$_FILES</code> and <code>$_POST</code> before any PHP script runs, so a firewall written in PHP (for example via <code>auto_prepend_file</code>) is too late.</li>
            <li><code>opcache.preload</code> runs once when the server starts, never per request.</li>
            <li>A network firewall sees bytes on the wire, not how PHP will interpret them.</li>
            <li>A compiled extension runs inside PHP's own upload parser, so it can count parts as PHP reads them and stop the request early.</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:.95rem">The incident details above come from public reports. We are checking them against the official advisories (GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w, GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp, php.net) and will correct this page if they differ.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="versions">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Version support: PHP 5.4 to 8.5</h2>
      <p>Howdah ships one build per PHP minor version. A version marked <strong>Tested</strong> has a passing lab result{ranDate && <> (run {ranDate})</>}; a version marked <strong>Expected</strong> shares a generation's code path with a tested version but has not been independently run, so it is not claimed as a test result. The <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a> has the full per-server-type detail.</p>
      <ul class="matrix" aria-label="PHP version support">
        {rows.map((r: any) => (
          <li>
            <span class="v">{r.version ?? r.minor}</span>
            {r.version
              ? <span class="pill ok">Tested</span>
              : <span class="pill na">Expected</span>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="legend">Linux x86_64 and aarch64, non-thread-safe builds. The exact per-architecture binaries available are whatever the release build produced — see the <a href="/install/">install picker</a> and the release assets.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="compare" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How Howdah compares</h2>
      <p>Howdah does one narrow thing well: it refuses resource-exhaustion request shapes from inside PHP, on versions nothing else still covers. It is not a general-purpose WAF. Here is where it sits among tools people reach for, as of {compareAsOf}.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison with other tools (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">How Howdah compares with other PHP protection tools, as of {compareAsOf}</caption>
          <thead>
